\f2\b\fs56 NSCharacterSet Class Cluster\

\fs70 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\fs28\li200\fc0\cf0 Class Cluster Description\

\fs14 \

\f0\b0\fs28\li1500 An NSCharacterSet object represents a set of Unicode characters. NSString and NSScanner objects use NSCharacterSets to group characters together for searching operations, so that they can find any of a particular set of characters during a search. The cluster's two public classes, NSCharacterSet and NSMutableCharacterSet, declare the programmatic interface for static and dynamic character sets, respectively.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 The objects you create using these classes are referred to as 
\i character set objects
\i0  (and when no confusion will result, merely as 
\i character sets
\i0 ). Because of the nature of class clusters, character set objects aren't actual instances of the NSCharacterSet or NSMutableCharacterSet classes but of one of their private subclasses. Although a character set object's class is private, its interface is public, as declared by these abstract superclasses, NSCharacterSet and NSMutableCharacterSet. (See ªClass Clustersº in the introduction to the Foundation Kit for more information on class clusters and on creating subclasses within a cluster.) The character set classes adopt the NSCopying and NSMutableCopying protocols, making it convenient to convert a character set of one type to the other.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Building a Character Set\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 NSCharacterSet defines class methods that return commonly used character sets, such as letters (uppercase or lowercase), decimal digits, whitespace, and so on. These ªstandardº character sets are always immutable, even if created by sending a message to NSMutableCharacterSet. See ªStandard Character Sets and Unicode Definitionsº below for more information on standard character sets.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 You can use a standard character set as a starting point for building a custom set by making a mutable copy of it and changing that. (You can also start from scratch by creating a mutable character set with 
\b alloc
\b0  and 
\b init
\b0  and adding characters to it.) For example, this fragment creates a character set containing letters, digits, and basic punctuation:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 NSMutableCharacterSet *workingSet;\
NSCharacterSet *finalCharSet;\
\
workingSet = [[NSCharacterSet alphanumericCharacterSet] mutableCopy];\
[workingSet addCharactersInString:@";:,."];\
finalCharSet = [workingSet copy];\
[workingSet release];\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 For performance reasons (explained in ªUsing a Character Setº), always finish by converting the working mutable character set into an immutable set. If you need to keep changing the character set after you've created it, of course, you should just use the mutable set.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 If your application frequently uses a custom character set, you'll want to save its definition in a resource file and load that instead of explicitly adding individual characters each time you need to create the set. You can save a character set by getting its bitmap representation (an NSData object) and saving that object to a file:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 NSString *filename;    /* Assume this exists. */\
NSString *absolutePath;\
NSData *charSetRep;\
BOOL result;\
\
absolutePath = [filename stringByStandardizingPath];\
charSetRep = [finalCharSet bitmapRepresentation];\
result = [charSetRep writeToFile:absolutePath atomically:YES];\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Character set filenames by convention use the extension 
\b .bitmap
\b0 . If you intend for others to use your character set files, you should follow this convention. To read a character set file with a 
\b .bitmap
\b0  extension, simply use the 
\b characterSetWithContentsOfFile:
\b0  method.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Using a Character Set\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 A character set object doesn't perform any tasks; it simply holds a set of character values to limit operations on strings. The NSString and NSScanner classes define methods that take NSCharacterSets as arguments to find any of several characters. For example, this code excerpt finds the range of the first uppercase letter in 
\b myString
\b0 :\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx2400\tx2880\tx3420\tx3940\tx4440\tx4920\tx7000\tx7500\tx8000\tx8500\tx9020\tx9520\tx10020\tx10520\tx11020\tx11500\f3\fs24\li1900\fc0\cf0 NSString *myString = @"some text in an NSString...";\
NSRange letterRange;\
\
letterRange = [myString rangeOfCharacterFromSet:[NSCharacterSet\
    uppercaseLetterCharacterSet]];\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 After this fragment executes, 
\b letterRange.location
\b0  is equal to the index of the first ªNº in ªNSStringº after 
\b rangeOfCharacterFromSet:
\b0  is invoked. If the first letter of the string were ªSº then 
\b letterRange.location
\b0  would be 0.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Because character sets often participate in performance-critical code, you should be aware of the aspects of their use that can affect the performance of your application. Mutable character sets are generally much more expensive than immutable character sets. They consume more memory and are costly to invert (an operation often performed in scanning a string). Because of this, you should follow these guidelines:\

\fs14 \

\pard\tx1800\tx6300\tx6580\tx15140\fs28\fi-300\li1800\fc0\cf0 ·	Create as few mutable character sets as possible.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Cache character sets (in a global dictionary, perhaps) instead of continually recreating them.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	When creating a custom set that doesn't need to change after creation, make an immutable copy of the final character set for actual use, and dispose of the working mutable character set. Alternatively, create a character set file as described in ªBuilding a Character Setº and store it in your application's main bundle.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 ·	Similarly, avoid archiving character set objects; store them in character set files instead. Archiving can result in a character set being duplicated in different archive files, resulting in wasted disk space and duplicates in memory for each separate archive read.\

\fs14 \

\fs26 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 Standard Character Sets and Unicode Definitions\

\fs8 \

\fs6 \

\pard\tx3600\tx3980\tx4340\f0\b0\fs28\li1500\fc0\cf0 The standard character sets, such as that returned by 
\b letterCharacterSet
\b0 , are formally defined in terms of the normative and informative categories established by the Unicode standard, such as Uppercase Letter, Combining Mark, and so on. The formal definition of a standard character set is in most cases given as one or more of the categories defined in the standard. For example, the set returned by 
\b lowercaseLetterCharacterSet
\b0  include all characters in normative category Lowercase Letters, while the set returned by 
\b letterCharacterSet
\b0  includes the characters in all of the Letter categories.\

\fs14 \

\fs28 Note that the definitions of the categories themselves may change with new versions of the Unicode standard. You can download the files that define category membership from 
\b http://www.unicode.org/
\b0 . \

\fs14 \

